// THE BLESS LIST — deliberate openings in the Tiger's armour (design doc §13.6).
//
// "Each finding is either a real hole (FIX) or a deliberate opening (BLESS — turret ring, MG port,
// vision slits: historical weakspots consciously kept as gameplay, with exact knowledge of how
// they'll play at every gun in the game). The bless-list is where weakspots are DECIDED, not
// discovered by players as bugs."
//
// The producer is `src/ballistics/fuzz.rs`; the consumer is its CI gate and
// `cargo run --bin ballistic_fuzzer`. A corridor to crew or ammunition that no entry here covers
// FAILS THE BUILD by name. An entry that no corridor comes in through is reported as stale.
//
// FIELDS
//   name    — what the opening IS, in the vocabulary a designer would use.
//   center  — TANK-LOCAL metres of the region on the tank's bounding box the corridors come in
//             through. Local `-Z` is the front, `+Z` the rear, `+Y` up; the origin is the tank root.
//             This is the same descriptor `Region::entry_local` reports, so a finding can be pasted
//             here directly.
//   radius  — tank-local metres around `center` this blessing covers.
//   admits  — the smallest caliber (METRES) MEASURED to get through. Not a threshold the gate
//             enforces — a record of what was accepted, so "how will this play against a 20 mm"
//             has an answer that was measured rather than guessed.
//   reason  — why this hole is allowed to exist. Required, and read by a test: a blessing without a
//             reason is a decision nobody recorded.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STATUS 2026-08-07: EMPTY, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT.
//
// 200 000 rays at the bound `tiger_1` found NO corridor reaching crew or ammunition below the gate
// floor (7.92 mm capability = 8.2 reference-mm). §13.7's "remaining geometry sin — the super-caliber
// accidental corridor" is not present in this asset: the turret ring, the MG ports and the vision
// slits are modelled as SOLID cast/steel rather than as openings, so the disc law has no geometry to
// grade them against and there is nothing to bless.
//
// The thinnest routes the same run measured, for the record (axis-ray reference-mm to first contact
// with the volume — all of them THROUGH armour, none of them holes):
//
//     Commander 18.1 · Gunner 22.7 · Loader 23.3 · Ammo_L_1 24.1 · Ammo_R_0 24.2
//     Ammo_L_0 25.0 · Ammo_R_1 25.0 · Driver 25.0 · BowGunner 25.0
//
// The commander's 18.1 is the one number below the tank's own thinnest authored plate (25 mm roof).
// It is a graded thin spot around the cupola, admitted by a 37 mm gun at η = 1.0 and refused by
// everything smaller — flagged for a modelling decision, deliberately NOT blessed here, because a
// blessing is a statement that somebody decided it and nobody has.
//
// So: adding the FIRST row to this file is a design act. Copy the region descriptor out of the
// fuzzer's report, state the measured admitting caliber, and write down why the hole is wanted.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(
    openings: [],
)
